  "functions": [
    "FUNC.TEXT"
  ],
  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "In TEXT, an @ applied to a number gives the General display, not the full number",
  "statement": "A format of @ or @! on a number gives what General gives, not the digits of the number. TEXT(123456789012345,\"@\") is 1.23457E+14 and TEXT(1234567890.5,\"@\") is 1234567891. A quoted @ is a literal: TEXT(x,\"\"\"@\"\"\") is @ for a positive x, and -@ for a negative one, because the ordinary sign rule applies. In a format with four sections, @ is the text section and numbers use the others: TEXT(123456789012345,\"0;0;0;@\") shows all 15 digits, and TEXT(-123456789012345,\"0;0;0;@\") shows them with no minus sign, because the second section has none.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "30 recorded readings: six numbers against the formats @, General, quoted @, @! and 0;0;0;@. @ and @! equalled General for all six numbers.",
  "inferred": null,
